1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,360
Got $10,000 for one fish?

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:11,480
From the open seas, to the cannery, to the school lunchbox,

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,980
it's the fish that means so much to so many.

4
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:19,000
Powerfully built, they're sleek, and they're fast.

5
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,040
If there ever was a sexy fish, this is it.

6
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,980
But due to overfishing, the most prized of their species is in trouble.

7
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:29,640
Wherever they swim, they're the most sought-after fish in the world.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,880
Today, people across the globe are working to save these ocean giants.

9
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:37,300
But can they be bred in captivity?

10
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,820
Now, it's Tuna on Modern Marvels.

11
00:01:00,029 --> 00:01:04,820
Every morning in seaports and fishing villages throughout the world, fishermen are gearing

12
00:01:04,821 --> 00:01:10,720
up, collecting bait, and heading out.

13
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:18,100
All with the same goal in mind, reeling in that most powerful and delectable of fish,

14
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:20,100
tuna.

15
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:29,580
In an average year, nearly 4 million tons of tuna are hauled aboard ship.

16
00:01:29,581 --> 00:01:34,580
That's almost one and a half pounds for every man, woman and child on earth,

17
00:01:34,581 --> 00:01:37,580
making it the most popular food fish in the world.

18
00:01:37,581 --> 00:01:40,580
What most people think of tunas is as a tuna sandwich.

19
00:01:40,581 --> 00:01:45,580
Well, actually, these are magnificent creatures, but people don't know about them.

20
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:51,580
The tuna is probably the best designed fish in the world.

21
00:01:51,581 --> 00:01:55,580
If you had to design something that was perfect at moving through the water,

22
00:01:55,581 --> 00:01:58,580
you would end up designing a tuna.

23
00:01:58,581 --> 00:02:06,380
The name tuna is derived from the Greek verb tuno, meaning rush, which is appropriate given

24
00:02:06,381 --> 00:02:11,200
the tuna's remarkable swimming ability.

25
00:02:11,201 --> 00:02:15,860
Bullet shaped and built for speed and distance, some species have been clocked at more than

26
00:02:15,861 --> 00:02:18,780
50 miles per hour.

27
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:24,280
When tuna swim rapidly, their fins retract into grooves, and even their eyes form a smooth

28
00:02:24,281 --> 00:02:28,360
surface with the rest of the head.

29
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:32,620
are found in all major bodies of water, except the polar seas.

30
00:02:32,621 --> 00:02:35,860
These nomads roam incredibly long distances,

31
00:02:35,861 --> 00:02:40,600
often making transoceanic migrations in a matter of weeks.

32
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:44,600
Like sharks, tuna never rest.

33
00:02:44,601 --> 00:02:46,560
Their demand for oxygen requires swimming

34
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:50,360
one body length per second.

35
00:02:50,361 --> 00:02:52,600
Some species, such as the bluefin,

36
00:02:52,601 --> 00:02:56,200
are warm-blooded, enabling them to maintain a body temperature

37
00:02:56,201 --> 00:02:59,040
slightly higher than the surrounding water.

38
00:02:59,041 --> 00:03:03,080
This added heat gives an extra boost to their already powerful muscles.

39
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:08,440
Tunas are the most remarkable bony fish in all of the sea.

40
00:03:08,441 --> 00:03:14,740
There's over 20,000 species of fish and tunas are the pinnacle of bony fish evolution.

41
00:03:14,741 --> 00:03:18,440
There are at least a dozen species of tuna.

42
00:03:18,441 --> 00:03:24,100
They range in size from the canning tunas, such as albacore and skipjack, to the largest

43
00:03:24,101 --> 00:03:28,600
and fastest of them all, the bluefin.

44
00:03:28,601 --> 00:03:30,500
We call it the Porsche of the oceans.

45
00:03:30,501 --> 00:03:33,000
This is an animal that is as big as a Porsche.

46
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:35,480
They get up to 1,500 pounds.

47
00:03:35,481 --> 00:03:37,280
It's as fast as a Porsche.

48
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:41,680
These are incredibly hydrodynamic, very amazing animals in the wild.

49
00:03:41,681 --> 00:03:49,080
And it's as expensive as a Porsche with one animal going for more than $100,000.

50
00:03:49,081 --> 00:03:54,020
species of tuna are predators, often feeding on smaller schooling fishes.

51
00:03:54,021 --> 00:03:59,840
They're near the top of the food chain with few natural enemies.

52
00:03:59,841 --> 00:04:05,360
Tunas do have predators. They'll be fed on by large sharks and probably one of

53
00:04:05,361 --> 00:04:10,980
their most voracious predators are us, we humans. The tuna may be blessed with a

54
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:15,920
dynamic nature but it's surprisingly easy to catch.

55
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:21,100
The Portuguese and Italian fishers who came to America in the early 1900s

56
00:04:21,101 --> 00:04:27,280
brought with them one of the earliest methods known as jackpolling.

57
00:04:27,281 --> 00:04:32,080
A century later an independent collection of families out of San Diego

58
00:04:32,081 --> 00:04:36,080
still practice this technique.

59
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:43,760
They catch, can and distribute their own brand, American tuna.

60
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:47,760
This type of fishing right here is sport fishing on steroids.

61
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:48,760
It's the pinnacle of it.

62
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:51,760
This technique is a hundred-year-old technique.

63
00:04:51,761 --> 00:04:54,760
This is how the tuna industry started.

64
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,760
Every year from June to November,

65
00:04:58,761 --> 00:05:01,760
Jack Webster and his fellow fishers comb the waters of the Pacific

66
00:05:01,761 --> 00:05:03,760
where the albacore are biting.

67
00:05:03,761 --> 00:05:05,760
Their equipment is minimal.

68
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:11,760
So I have a ten-foot pole here with about a six-foot piece of line on it.

69
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:15,760
The stainless steel wire and the jig, that's all you have.

70
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:18,900
Tracking the tuna is part fish sense,

71
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:23,900
recognizing telltale signs, such as the presence of seabirds,

72
00:05:23,901 --> 00:05:25,500
and part electronic.

73
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:28,600
The boat is equipped with a depth sounder

74
00:05:28,601 --> 00:05:31,760
that uses sound waves to detect fish beneath the craft,

75
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,340
sonar, which can track them in front of the boat

76
00:05:34,341 --> 00:05:37,340
or on either side, and satellite imaging.

77
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:41,260
Once the fishers have located a school,

78
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:48,320
a designated chummer begins tossing live bait usually anchovies or sardines to attract the tuna

79
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:55,280
jack bowlers descend into steel racks to get as close to the water as possible

80
00:05:56,640 --> 00:06:00,320
there's a foothold right here where you're going to put your right foot over it

81
00:06:00,321 --> 00:06:04,000
you want this rail on the rack right here where your knee is going to hit it

82
00:06:04,001 --> 00:06:11,360
one foot back to keep your balance you put the squid in the water this is as an artificial tail

83
00:06:11,361 --> 00:06:16,580
on it and this is what you'd be doing to give it a little action when the fish

84
00:06:16,581 --> 00:06:21,960
bites you make a v-shape here this arm goes right down to the pad to get the

85
00:06:21,961 --> 00:06:27,380
butt of the pole in the pad this arm goes forward the fish is on the hook you got

86
00:06:27,381 --> 00:06:33,600
both hands on it throw it up and it's on the deck then back in the water

87
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:39,040
jack bowlers bring in their fish one at a time always throwing the catch over

88
00:06:39,041 --> 00:06:40,040
their right shoulder.

89
00:06:40,041 --> 00:06:44,120
The hook is specially designed for a quick release.

90
00:06:44,121 --> 00:06:46,500
This hook is like an L-shaped.

91
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:48,500
It's not a full hook.

92
00:06:48,501 --> 00:06:51,980
And what happens is, well, you gotta keep steady pressure on it.

93
00:06:51,981 --> 00:06:55,160
When the fish is coming up over your head and you give it a little bit of slack, it'll

94
00:06:55,161 --> 00:06:58,660
fall out of the fish's mouth and the fish will fall on the deck.

95
00:06:58,661 --> 00:07:00,720
Then you can go back in the water.

96
00:07:00,721 --> 00:07:05,540
The Jack Polar's boat can hold up to 55 tons of tuna.

97
00:07:05,541 --> 00:07:08,200
Outstanding, man.

98
00:07:08,201 --> 00:07:11,200
Good job, boys.

99
00:07:11,201 --> 00:07:13,280
A brine solution freezes the fish

100
00:07:13,281 --> 00:07:15,080
before they're hauled on shore.

101
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,920
At times we feel like a dinosaur.

102
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:22,320
One of the biggest questions I get from the public at large

103
00:07:22,321 --> 00:07:24,920
is they can't believe that we still do this.

104
00:07:24,921 --> 00:07:27,680
Myself, I'm absolutely happy with what I do,

105
00:07:27,681 --> 00:07:29,520
and I'm at peace with it.

106
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:33,560
Tuna fisheries around the world

107
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:38,160
harvest hundreds of thousands of tons of albacore.

108
00:07:38,161 --> 00:07:42,360
Our fishery in the North Pacific averages 10,000.

109
00:07:42,361 --> 00:07:47,900
So we're a fraction of the total picture of tuna harvested.

110
00:07:47,901 --> 00:07:50,780
In fact, jack-polling accounts for less than 5%

111
00:07:50,781 --> 00:07:54,640
of the world tuna catch.

112
00:07:54,641 --> 00:07:56,920
Today, factory-sized commercial vessels

113
00:07:56,921 --> 00:08:01,520
catch the vast majority of tuna, using one of two methods.

114
00:08:01,521 --> 00:08:04,520
The first is longline fishing.

115
00:08:04,521 --> 00:08:09,820
This method uses thousands of baited hooks attached to extremely long fishing lines,

116
00:08:09,821 --> 00:08:13,160
some of them extending 40 to 50 miles.

117
00:08:13,161 --> 00:08:19,240
Longlining accounts for about 14% of the world's total tuna catch.

118
00:08:19,241 --> 00:08:21,980
Although it's very effective at catching tuna,

119
00:08:21,981 --> 00:08:26,820
longlining pulls in many non-targeted species as well.

120
00:08:26,821 --> 00:08:30,780
Longlines particularly have a high level of concern for us

121
00:08:30,781 --> 00:08:33,880
because of the bycatch of sharks, turtles, and seabirds.

122
00:08:33,881 --> 00:08:36,780
The second method is purse seining.

123
00:08:36,781 --> 00:08:40,080
It involves laying out a purse-shaped net, or seine,

124
00:08:40,081 --> 00:08:42,680
in a wide circle around a school of fish.

125
00:08:42,681 --> 00:08:46,680
By pulling a cable through weighted rings at the bottom of the net,

126
00:08:46,681 --> 00:08:50,580
the fishers draw the seine into a purse, closing off the bottom

127
00:08:50,581 --> 00:08:52,680
and capturing the catch inside.

128
00:08:52,681 --> 00:08:58,280
The massive net can entrap and hold up to 3,000 tuna in a single toss.

129
00:08:58,281 --> 00:09:04,700
purse seining accounts for roughly 60% of all the tuna landed

130
00:09:04,701 --> 00:09:12,560
it also produces a significant bycatch most notably dolphins since they often

131
00:09:12,561 --> 00:09:21,500
swim above schools of yellowfin tuna from the late 1950s when commercial fishermen

132
00:09:21,501 --> 00:09:28,500
first began purse saining until the early 1970s. An estimated 350,000 dolphins died

133
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:30,330
each year in purse sains.

134
00:09:30,331 --> 00:09:34,960
But with technological advances such as escape hatches for the dolphins

135
00:09:34,961 --> 00:09:40,260
and passage of the Marine Mammal Protection Act in 1972, that number has been dramatically

136
00:09:40,261 --> 00:09:46,436
reduced. Still, thousands of dolphins are killed annually in this practice.

137
00:09:46,437 --> 00:09:47,260
We need to

138
00:09:47,261 --> 00:09:51,400
We need to get away from longlining, purse seining, these destructive methods of fishing,

139
00:09:51,401 --> 00:09:56,920
in favor of more selective methods of fishing like hook and line and so forth.

140
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:05,520
California's Monterey Bay Aquarium has taken an active role in educating the public about fishing practices.

141
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:14,980
Its Seafood Watch program is designed in part to help consumers identify which fish are caught by sustainable methods and which are not.

142
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:26,260
The aquarium also houses the only exhibit in North America that includes bluefin and yellowfin tuna.

143
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:32,120
Eleven different species share the million-gallon tank,

144
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:38,420
but the bluefin, each weighing several hundred pounds, are clearly the stars.

145
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:41,900
They inspire awe among our visitors.

146
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:46,640
They love seeing these tunas in a tank that simulates what they look like in the wild.

147
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:49,880
It's not an animal you'll see in captivity too often.

148
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:52,500
It does require a little extra care and handling.

149
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:55,760
They're warm-bodied, so they have these really high metabolic rates,

150
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:57,480
and so they have these big appetites.

151
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:03,040
In the wild, bluefin's attain their enormous size

152
00:11:03,041 --> 00:11:07,240
by gorging themselves on fish, crustaceans, squid, and eel.

153
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:11,620
In the aquarium, four times a week,

154
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:14,520
they feast on 25 pounds of sardines,

155
00:11:14,521 --> 00:11:19,280
40 pounds of squid and 15 pounds of a vitamin-rich gel.

156
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:22,300
Tunas are a little picky.

157
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:24,360
They usually don't take the gel as well,

158
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:27,320
so we'll tend to feed the gel and the squid first.

159
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:29,020
And they really like the sardines,

160
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:31,680
so we'll feed the sardines last after we make sure they get

161
00:11:31,681 --> 00:11:34,640
all the nutrients and vitamins they need in the gel food.

162
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:39,220
The tuna's ultra-efficient swimming ability

163
00:11:39,221 --> 00:11:42,360
has inspired the U.S. Navy to commission construction

164
00:11:42,361 --> 00:11:45,320
of a robotic tuna for surveillance missions.

165
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:48,720
Tuna will return on Modern Marvels.

166
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:54,040
We now return to Tuna on Modern Marvels.

167
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:00,940
Tuna is the most widely consumed fish in the American diet.

168
00:12:01,540 --> 00:12:03,040
Whether it's cooked on a grill,

169
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:05,620
made into a sandwich,

170
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:07,420
or sliced for sashimi,

171
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:11,600
the average American eats nearly three pounds of it every year.

172
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:14,360
Most of it is canned.

173
00:12:14,361 --> 00:12:19,360
We eat a billion pounds of canned and pouched tuna every year.

174
00:12:19,361 --> 00:12:24,360
That's more than a third of the total seafood consumed in the U.S.

175
00:12:24,361 --> 00:12:31,360
Surprisingly, America's tuna industry began quite by accident in 1903.

176
00:12:31,361 --> 00:12:36,360
In the early 20th century, tuna weren't caught very often and weren't canned very often.

177
00:12:36,361 --> 00:12:45,220
And the story is told that a sardine fisherman, unable to catch enough sardines to support his own cannery,

178
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:53,080
caught some albacore, caught some smaller tuna, and canned them, and they became very popular throughout America.

179
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:56,180
Suddenly they found something else that Americans would eat.

180
00:12:57,280 --> 00:13:04,440
Within a decade, nine plants were in operation along the Pacific coast, producing 115,000 cases annually.

181
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:12,760
During the First World War, American doughboys in Europe needed a convenient protein-rich

182
00:13:12,761 --> 00:13:17,640
food, and canned tuna proved a perfect solution.

183
00:13:17,641 --> 00:13:19,864
By the 1950s, the U.S.

184
00:13:19,865 --> 00:13:26,840
had become the largest producer and consumer of canned tuna products.

185
00:13:26,841 --> 00:13:32,020
One of the earliest canneries, and the only major tuna cannery still operating in America,

186
00:13:32,021 --> 00:13:33,020
is Bumblebee.

187
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:36,720
The company originally started as a canner of salmon products.

188
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:45,920
By probably 1940, 1945, the company moved to a canner of tuna as a predominant line offering to consumers.

189
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:53,720
Here in this Santa Fe Springs, California cannery, tuna is the only fish on the menu.

190
00:13:54,540 --> 00:13:55,240
Lots of it.

191
00:13:56,460 --> 00:14:00,740
This Santa Fe Springs tuna processing facility is the most efficient in the world.

192
00:14:00,741 --> 00:14:03,780
We run 200 tons of fish every single day.

193
00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:06,620
We put 40,000 cases out the door,

194
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:11,520
so fish comes in one door and 40,000 cases goes out the next every single day.

195
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:16,000
Bumblebee tuna comes from waters throughout the world,

196
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:18,120
including Southeast Asia.

197
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:23,100
The catch consists of albacore, skipjack, big eye, and yellowfin.

198
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:29,780
By the time it reaches the U.S., it's in frozen slabs wrapped in plastic.

199
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:37,740
That's after being headed, tailed, cooked and deboned in overseas processing plants.

200
00:14:37,741 --> 00:14:43,740
So here in the United States, we're not dealing with the whole fish, we're dealing with the loin bag.

201
00:14:43,741 --> 00:14:49,740
That loin bag contains the fillets of the fish. That's what we put into the cans.

202
00:14:49,741 --> 00:14:53,740
Before being canned, the tuna must be thawed.

203
00:14:53,741 --> 00:14:59,740
That takes place inside a bumblebee's uniquely designed thaw tunnel.

204
00:14:59,741 --> 00:15:05,700
What goes in frozen solid comes out at 50 to 55 degrees.

205
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:12,320
Then it's off to the fillers, seven separate lines operating 24-7.

206
00:15:13,940 --> 00:15:21,900
Some are designed to run albacore or white tuna, while others process light tuna, made mostly from skipjack.

207
00:15:21,901 --> 00:15:29,520
The fillers are programmed to pack 5-ounce steel cans with either chunk tuna, made up

208
00:15:29,521 --> 00:15:34,740
of smaller pieces, or the larger pieced solid variety.

209
00:15:34,741 --> 00:15:37,780
Each filler can handle as many as 600 cans per minute.

210
00:15:37,781 --> 00:15:43,460
Two million cans, two million of those little 5-ounce cans that you see in the grocery store

211
00:15:43,461 --> 00:15:45,680
every day.

212
00:15:45,681 --> 00:15:50,780
Then it's time to add liquid, whether it's oil, a vegetable broth, or just plain water.

213
00:15:50,781 --> 00:15:57,780
The filled cans then pass along the assembly line to a seamer, where they're vacuum sealed.

214
00:15:57,781 --> 00:16:05,780
The seamer dispenses lids, or ends, at a rate of anywhere from 100 to 800 cans a minute.

215
00:16:05,781 --> 00:16:11,780
Powerful magnetic elevators then transport the sealed cans into large metal baskets.

216
00:16:11,781 --> 00:16:18,780
These baskets are loaded 12 at a time into giant pressure cookers, known as retorts,

217
00:16:18,781 --> 00:16:21,800
where the fish is cooked and sterilized.

218
00:16:21,801 --> 00:16:24,960
Each retort can cook up to 12,000 cans at a time.

219
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:29,240
We have nine retorts that we load our product into.

220
00:16:29,241 --> 00:16:32,960
We run it up to between 230 and 240 degrees

221
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:35,820
for about two hours, and then it's cooked and ready to go.

222
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:40,700
After cooling, the cans pass through an X-ray machine

223
00:16:40,701 --> 00:16:42,360
that checks for bones or metals.

224
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:47,100
Only then will they be fed into the labeling,

225
00:16:47,101 --> 00:16:49,640
which slaps the familiar Bumblebee logo on the cans.

226
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:54,560
All Bumblebee cans also bear the Dolphins Safe label.

227
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:57,500
These labels can be traced to 1990,

228
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:00,560
when the world's three largest tuna companies,

229
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:03,420
Starkist, Bumblebee, and Chicken of the Sea,

230
00:17:04,180 --> 00:17:07,120
agreed to stop purchasing, processing, and selling tuna

231
00:17:07,121 --> 00:17:09,820
caught by intentional chasing and netting of dolphins.

232
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:14,080
We have the National Marine Fishery release,

233
00:17:14,081 --> 00:17:16,440
and that is a sign-off from the captain

234
00:17:16,441 --> 00:17:19,440
that says that there has been no dolphin caught with that fish.

235
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:23,980
Bumblebee ranks number two nationwide in total sales,

236
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:28,300
next to Starkist, accounting for roughly 31% of the market.

237
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:32,960
And while sales of canned tuna have been on a general decline

238
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:33,960
over the past decade,

239
00:17:34,880 --> 00:17:37,600
there has been a spike during the recent economic crisis

240
00:17:37,601 --> 00:17:41,080
as more and more people began bringing their lunch to work.

241
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:46,200
Tuna for generations has been one of those wholesome family experiences.

242
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:49,440
It's versatile, it's delicious,

243
00:17:49,441 --> 00:17:53,440
it's loaded with incredible health benefits.

244
00:17:53,441 --> 00:17:56,440
Those benefits include high protein, low fat,

245
00:17:56,441 --> 00:17:59,440
and a rich source of omega-3 fatty acids

246
00:17:59,441 --> 00:18:02,440
that have been shown to reduce the risk of heart disease.

247
00:18:02,441 --> 00:18:07,440
What we're learning today is consumers just aren't getting enough fish in their diet.

248
00:18:07,441 --> 00:18:10,440
While eating tuna does have enormous health benefits,

249
00:18:10,441 --> 00:18:13,440
consumers should be aware of some guidelines.

250
00:18:13,441 --> 00:18:20,020
Tunas are predators, and so we see that they do feed on a variety of other animals, smaller

251
00:18:20,021 --> 00:18:26,100
fishes, squids, and a lot of toxins that might be present in animals that they're feeding

252
00:18:26,101 --> 00:18:29,700
on, like mercury, can be passed up the food chain to these tunas.

253
00:18:29,701 --> 00:18:34,320
For most people, the risk from mercury by eating fish is not a concern.

254
00:18:34,321 --> 00:18:39,940
However, the Food and Drug Administration and the Environmental Protection Agency do recommend

255
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:42,640
that women who may become pregnant or are pregnant,

256
00:18:43,180 --> 00:18:44,740
nursing mothers and young children,

257
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:47,620
should limit their consumption to 12 ounces a week

258
00:18:47,621 --> 00:18:49,200
of a variety of fish,

259
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:51,760
including those that are lower in mercury,

260
00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:53,320
like canned light tuna.

261
00:18:54,100 --> 00:18:57,260
In the U.S., over one-half of canned tuna,

262
00:18:57,860 --> 00:19:00,160
52% is used in sandwiches,

263
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:03,380
another 22% is used in salads,

264
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:07,500
while 15% is used in casseroles and helpers.

265
00:19:07,501 --> 00:19:11,340
Tuna will return on Modern Marvelous.

266
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:16,320
We now return to Tuna on Modern Marvelous.

267
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:21,660
Tuna has no trouble crossing demographic lines.

268
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:25,080
You can enjoy an inexpensive tuna sandwich

269
00:19:25,081 --> 00:19:30,820
or plunk down $75 for a single slice of tuna at a high-end sushi bar.

270
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:34,180
And at this Japanese fish market,

271
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:37,200
you'll find some of the priciest tuna in the world.

272
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:44,500
No place sells more fish than the Tsukiji market in Tokyo.

273
00:19:44,501 --> 00:19:49,500
Four billion dollars annually flows through the market.

274
00:19:49,501 --> 00:20:00,500
It is 52 acres of every conceivable creature that could possibly live in the sea.

275
00:20:00,501 --> 00:20:09,180
They sell clams, crabs, oysters, scallops, mussels, every conceivable kind of fish, seaweed,

276
00:20:09,181 --> 00:20:10,920
fish eggs, everything.

277
00:20:10,921 --> 00:20:20,100
But the major attraction, if you will, at the Skiji fish market are the tuna auctions.

278
00:20:20,101 --> 00:20:28,260
Six days a week, in several rooms the size of high school gymnasiums, an estimated 1,000

279
00:20:28,261 --> 00:20:32,320
giant bluefin are auctioned off.

280
00:20:32,321 --> 00:20:35,620
The fish arrive in Japan from oceans around the world,

281
00:20:35,621 --> 00:20:39,240
often within 24 hours of being caught.

282
00:20:39,241 --> 00:20:41,220
Some come packed in ice.

283
00:20:41,221 --> 00:20:43,180
Others have been flash frozen aboard ship.

284
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:48,660
More and more fish come here.

285
00:20:48,661 --> 00:20:51,240
So their prices are determined here.

286
00:20:51,241 --> 00:20:55,440
And now it is especially so with more than 100 countries

287
00:20:55,441 --> 00:20:57,840
exporting to Tsukiji.

288
00:20:57,841 --> 00:21:04,840
The Japanese believe that the most wonderful food item you can possibly consume

289
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:10,700
is the belly meat of a bluefin tuna, the red, fatty meat of a bluefin tuna.

290
00:21:10,701 --> 00:21:13,460
It commands extraordinary prices in Japan.

291
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:16,460
It commands extraordinary prices elsewhere.

292
00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:22,160
That belly meat, known as toro, is the most expensive part of the tuna.

293
00:21:22,900 --> 00:21:27,780
Used for sushi and sashimi, it has made bluefin the most sought after of all tunas.

294
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:32,435
Buyers for the top sushi chefs in the world begin arriving at 4 a.m.

295
00:21:32,436 --> 00:21:35,340
to inspect the bluefin that are on display.

296
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:39,380
They roll the tuna on their side, looking in the belly.

297
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:42,720
The color of the meat indicates the fat content.

298
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:44,460
The fattier, the better.

299
00:21:45,940 --> 00:21:48,160
The buyers use flashlights to examine the meat.

300
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:54,720
They also rub small pieces between their thumb and forefingers to get a sense of the oil content.

301
00:21:54,721 --> 00:22:04,480
The auctioneer and buyers have a sign language all their own.

302
00:22:04,481 --> 00:22:12,480
Well, this is thousand yen or ten thousand yen or hundred thousand yen per kilo.

303
00:22:12,481 --> 00:22:19,220
When you have your palm outwards, that means selling, and your palm inwards is to buy.

304
00:22:19,221 --> 00:22:26,380
A single bluefin generally sells from anywhere between $2,000 and $20,000.

305
00:22:26,381 --> 00:22:30,380
We now return to Tuna on Modern Marvels.

306
00:22:39,980 --> 00:22:48,760
In 2001, a bluefin was auctioned at Tsukiji for the all-time record, $173,600.

307
00:22:49,220 --> 00:22:56,220
Tuna Nori Ida buys tuna from more than 450 establishments in Japan.

308
00:22:56,221 --> 00:23:04,220
Tuna is the main dish for sushi restaurants, which means they cannot go without it, no matter what happens.

309
00:23:04,221 --> 00:23:10,220
You cannot say, sorry, we don't have tuna today. That would be taboo.

310
00:23:10,221 --> 00:23:18,220
While the fatty belly meat is used for toro, the rest of the fish is sliced and sold as cheaper cuts.

311
00:23:18,221 --> 00:23:27,220
In Japan, even the least expensive sushi, available in strip malls and grocery stores, is made from bluefin.

312
00:23:27,221 --> 00:23:31,220
But in America, we don't eat that much bluefin tuna.

313
00:23:31,221 --> 00:23:40,220
The tuna you get in sushi and sashimi and tuna steaks and tuna carpaccio in America is usually yellowfin or big eye tuna.

314
00:23:40,221 --> 00:23:46,220
The Japanese aren't the first to discover the virtues of bluefin tuna.

315
00:23:46,221 --> 00:23:46,921
tuna.

316
00:23:46,922 --> 00:23:55,060
In ancient Rome, soldiers marched into battle with their kit bags and stomachs filled

317
00:23:55,061 --> 00:23:57,443
with dried bluefin tuna.

318
00:23:57,444 --> 00:24:03,700
2,000 years later, big game fishermen such as authors Zane Gray

319
00:24:03,701 --> 00:24:07,100
and Ernest Hemingway discovered the thrill of the hunt.

320
00:24:07,760 --> 00:24:10,513
This is the beginning of sport fishing.

321
00:24:10,514 --> 00:24:13,760
The tuna was probably the first fish caught as

322
00:24:13,761 --> 00:24:18,320
sport fish because prior to that people had caught fish that they were gonna eat

323
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:25,760
at the turn of the 20th century the bluefin's fatty red meat was deemed inedible many sport

324
00:24:25,761 --> 00:24:31,600
fishermen would actually pay to have the bluefin's worthless carcass carted away after posing for

325
00:24:31,601 --> 00:24:40,480
photographs oh there we go oh i got it it doesn't feel big sport fishing obviously didn't do the tuna

326
00:24:40,481 --> 00:24:46,800
population that much harm but what happened over time is first of all people started eating bluefin

327
00:24:46,801 --> 00:24:52,420
tuna and then commercial fishermen figured out a way to catch them in very large numbers it's only

328
00:24:52,421 --> 00:24:58,720
within the past 30 years that the demand for bluefin tuna has reached staggering heights there are

329
00:24:58,721 --> 00:25:03,580
countries throughout the Mediterranean in fact every country in the Mediterranean has a tuna

330
00:25:03,581 --> 00:25:09,460
fishery and the tuna are caught at a young age, they're purse-samed, they're put in pens,

331
00:25:09,820 --> 00:25:16,260
they're fed until they are adult size and then they're killed and virtually all of them shipped

332
00:25:16,261 --> 00:25:21,920
to Japan. This all-out assault upon the bluefin has led to dire consequences.

333
00:25:23,420 --> 00:25:28,740
This method of fishing has one serious problem and that is if you catch half-grown tuna,

334
00:25:28,741 --> 00:25:32,120
you're catching the tuna before they are old enough to breed.

335
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:35,540
And if you catch the breeding population and remove it,

336
00:25:35,720 --> 00:25:37,800
you're not going to get much in the way of reproduction

337
00:25:37,801 --> 00:25:39,340
if there's no fish left to breed.

338
00:25:40,620 --> 00:25:43,580
Tuna requires specific conditions in order to breed

339
00:25:43,581 --> 00:25:45,420
and won't do it here.

340
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:49,720
So what has happened to the Mediterranean tuna population,

341
00:25:50,080 --> 00:25:50,980
it has crashed.

342
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:54,920
Fundamentally, bluefin tuna are just too valuable for their own good.

343
00:25:54,921 --> 00:26:01,000
Whenever a fish is worth $100,000 or more, a single animal,

344
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:04,400
I think it's fair to say we're going to see enormous pressure

345
00:26:04,401 --> 00:26:08,120
to fish that species right down to commercial extinction,

346
00:26:08,500 --> 00:26:10,460
and that is what's happened with bluefin tuna.

347
00:26:12,100 --> 00:26:14,740
International fishing quotas have been largely ignored,

348
00:26:15,740 --> 00:26:18,460
as have laws outlawing the use of spotter planes.

349
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:22,040
The situation has become so dire

350
00:26:22,041 --> 00:26:25,120
But even this veteran fish buyer acknowledges the problem.

351
00:26:27,340 --> 00:26:33,160
In my opinion, we should limit our catch to about 50% all over the world.

352
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:38,120
If we care about our children and their children, that's something we have to do.

353
00:26:40,020 --> 00:26:45,020
Bluefin tuna are one of the poster children for overfishing.

354
00:26:45,280 --> 00:26:50,020
The population of bluefin tuna has declined by more than 90% in the last several decades.

355
00:26:50,021 --> 00:26:52,360
the animal really has its back against the wall.

356
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:59,920
The Japanese import roughly 36% of the total world tuna catch,

357
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:03,580
but nearly 80% of the bluefin catch.

358
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:07,500
Tuna will return on Modern Marvels.

359
00:27:08,600 --> 00:27:11,920
We now return to Tuna on Modern Marvels.

360
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:16,760
With the all-too-real threat

361
00:27:16,761 --> 00:27:19,560
that the bluefin tuna may one day become extinct,

362
00:27:20,020 --> 00:27:22,020
People are fighting back.

363
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:30,020
Dr. Barbara Block and a dedicated team of scientists in Monterey, California,

364
00:27:30,021 --> 00:27:32,020
have been at the forefront.

365
00:27:35,020 --> 00:27:38,020
Here at the Tuna Research and Conservation Center,

366
00:27:38,021 --> 00:27:41,020
we have tunas in captivity, and this allows us to study

367
00:27:41,021 --> 00:27:44,020
how the tuna works as a machine.

368
00:27:44,021 --> 00:27:48,020
It allows us to understand better the biology of the bluefin tuna,

369
00:27:48,021 --> 00:27:53,280
And we're literally just opening the door to some of the basic questions of what makes this animal tick.

370
00:27:54,620 --> 00:28:00,940
Understanding what makes bluefin tick can mean anything from studying the amount of energy the tuna utilizes per tailbeat

371
00:28:00,941 --> 00:28:05,540
to finding ways of determining its sex and place of origin.

372
00:28:07,020 --> 00:28:13,380
The center includes an 8,000 square foot facility that houses several tanks filled with bluefins.

373
00:28:13,381 --> 00:28:14,081
Dr.

374
00:28:14,082 --> 00:28:23,100
Block and her team have pioneered the electronic tagging of marine fish species in the wild.

375
00:28:24,380 --> 00:28:31,060
One of the great mysteries of the open sea is where do giant tunas go?

376
00:28:31,340 --> 00:28:36,220
We've had a difficult time following them because they breathe with gills and that keeps them submerged.

377
00:28:36,221 --> 00:28:46,583
So to follow Big Fish in the Sea, what we've done is we've taken small computers that are powered with batteries that we actually insert inside the tuna, a sensor

378
00:28:46,584 --> 00:28:53,300
stock comes out from the tuna, and we're able to actually map the journeys of tunas for up to five years.

379
00:28:54,480 --> 00:28:57,222
Every year for the past decade and a half, Dr.

380
00:28:57,223 --> 00:29:03,480
Block and a team of scientists and fishers have taken to the Atlantic and Pacific in the name of science.

381
00:29:03,481 --> 00:29:10,040
Through their Tag-A-Giant Foundation, the team has caught and tagged more than a thousand bluefin tuna.

382
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:14,580
They use two different types of tags.

383
00:29:15,380 --> 00:29:22,740
The first, known as archival tags, are implanted in the abdomens of the tuna and programmed to record data at set intervals.

384
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:28,080
Sensors on the body of the tag record the depth and internal body temperature of the tuna.

385
00:29:28,081 --> 00:29:35,080
while sensors on the stalk record water temperature and light levels.

386
00:29:35,081 --> 00:29:39,080
The main limitation of the archival tags is that in order to retrieve the data,

387
00:29:39,081 --> 00:29:45,080
the fish must be caught and the fisher must return the tag to the center.

388
00:29:45,081 --> 00:29:50,080
A $1,000 reward is offered to anyone returning a tag from the Atlantic.

389
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:55,080
A $500 reward is offered for each tag retrieved from the Pacific.

390
00:29:55,081 --> 00:30:02,080
To date, archival tags have been returned from more than 20 different countries.

391
00:30:02,081 --> 00:30:09,080
The second type of tag, the pop-up satellite tag, records data similar to the archival tag,

392
00:30:09,081 --> 00:30:14,080
but its findings are accessible even if the fish isn't caught.

393
00:30:14,081 --> 00:30:19,080
Researchers secure the satellite tag to the back of the fish with a dart.

394
00:30:19,081 --> 00:30:23,080
At a pre-programmed time, anywhere from 30 days up to a year,

395
00:30:23,800 --> 00:30:27,660
an electrical impulse corrodes the wire that attaches the dart to the tag,

396
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:30,000
and the tag releases from the fish.

397
00:30:30,980 --> 00:30:35,100
It then floats to the surface and uploads the log data to a satellite.

398
00:30:35,840 --> 00:30:40,280
With this tag, we're almost taking back the mystery from the sea.

399
00:30:40,460 --> 00:30:42,700
We're finding out where the major corridors are,

400
00:30:43,280 --> 00:30:46,620
where the major deserts are and how the animals pass through them,

401
00:30:46,621 --> 00:30:49,380
where they come and congregate, where are the hot spots,

402
00:30:49,520 --> 00:30:54,180
and where are the places that are the equivalent of watering holes on the African savannah.

403
00:30:55,580 --> 00:30:59,260
Dr. Block's research has already yielded some startling results.

404
00:31:00,420 --> 00:31:03,860
She's discovered two distinct populations of Atlantic bluefin tuna,

405
00:31:04,760 --> 00:31:07,100
both of which swim incredible distances.

406
00:31:09,180 --> 00:31:12,520
During much of the year, the populations will intermingle,

407
00:31:12,680 --> 00:31:15,640
feeding together across a broad swath of the Atlantic.

408
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:19,320
But at certain times, during the spring and summer months,

409
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:22,900
mature fish will return to their native spawning grounds,

410
00:31:23,600 --> 00:31:26,100
either in the Mediterranean or the Gulf of Mexico.

411
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:29,760
So if we have high quotas, for example,

412
00:31:30,400 --> 00:31:32,760
over in the European side of the fishery

413
00:31:32,761 --> 00:31:36,000
and very low quotas on the North American side of the fishery,

414
00:31:36,180 --> 00:31:38,180
because the populations mix,

415
00:31:38,680 --> 00:31:41,420
we're finding that we have to work as common nations

416
00:31:41,421 --> 00:31:45,120
across the North Atlantic to actually protect the remaining resource.

417
00:31:45,121 --> 00:31:50,720
Thanks to the good work of our scientists here at the Tuna Research and Conservation

418
00:31:50,721 --> 00:31:54,040
Center, we know a lot more about where these animals go.

419
00:31:54,041 --> 00:31:57,240
We have the knowledge we need to manage them better.

420
00:31:57,241 --> 00:32:01,440
Now we just need to have the courage to put that knowledge to work.

421
00:32:01,441 --> 00:32:03,800
It may take more than courage.

422
00:32:03,801 --> 00:32:08,600
In 2008, the World Wildlife Foundation called for a complete halt to bluefin fishing in

423
00:32:08,601 --> 00:32:10,000
the Mediterranean.

424
00:32:10,001 --> 00:32:16,660
Instead, the International Commission for the Conservation of Atlantic Tunas, or ICAT,

425
00:32:16,661 --> 00:32:21,620
voted to approve quotas far in excess of those recommended by their own scientists.

426
00:32:21,621 --> 00:32:25,820
And so it's overfishing as usual.

427
00:32:25,821 --> 00:32:29,400
That's just got to stop, or we're going to lose this species on our watch.

428
00:32:29,401 --> 00:32:36,580
In the early 1900s, bluefin tuna was known as horse mackerel, and its fatty red meat

429
00:32:36,581 --> 00:32:39,640
was often ground into cat food.

430
00:32:39,641 --> 00:32:43,640
Tuna will return on Modern Marvels.

431
00:32:43,641 --> 00:32:49,460
We now return to Tuna on Modern Marvels.

432
00:32:49,461 --> 00:32:55,240
What happens if we fish the mighty bluefin to extinction?

433
00:32:55,241 --> 00:32:59,200
We learned a long time ago that taking out the big predators, the top predators in an

434
00:32:59,201 --> 00:33:02,400
ecosystem isn't particularly wise.

435
00:33:02,401 --> 00:33:06,640
We are fundamentally altering ocean ecosystems by removing the top predators.

436
00:33:06,641 --> 00:33:08,940
It's called fishing down the food chain.

437
00:33:08,941 --> 00:33:13,940
If we continue along that line, pretty soon the catch of the day is going to be jellyfish, not bluefin tuna.

438
00:33:14,940 --> 00:33:22,180
Some see hope for the bluefin and other tuna species in a modified version of the very process that is helping decimate their numbers.

439
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:29,240
If you think about the land for a moment, we stopped hunting wild animals on land for the market 100 years ago.

440
00:33:29,820 --> 00:33:32,054
Everything on land that we eat today is farmed.

441
00:33:32,055 --> 00:33:34,240
Very little, if anything, comes from the wild.

442
00:33:34,241 --> 00:33:38,040
We still market hunt wild animals for food in the oceans,

443
00:33:38,380 --> 00:33:40,580
but we at least ought to do it on a sustainable basis,

444
00:33:40,920 --> 00:33:41,940
and that's been problematic,

445
00:33:42,220 --> 00:33:44,060
particularly for species like bluefin tuna.

446
00:33:44,840 --> 00:33:48,380
So the future of seafood, it seems certain,

447
00:33:48,880 --> 00:33:50,780
is fish farming or aquaculture.

448
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:56,560
Aquaculture now accounts for 40% of the world's fish consumption,

449
00:33:57,460 --> 00:34:00,480
and tuna farms have sprung up in such diverse places

450
00:34:00,481 --> 00:34:03,780
as the Mediterranean, Mexico, and South Australia.

451
00:34:04,240 --> 00:34:14,020
A tuna farm is essentially a feedlot in the ocean, where tuna are taken from the wild,

452
00:34:14,021 --> 00:34:18,560
fattened in pens, slaughtered and sold.

453
00:34:18,561 --> 00:34:24,240
Due to the tuna's enormous appetites, it takes approximately 16 pounds of food to add one

454
00:34:24,241 --> 00:34:27,740
pound to each tuna.

455
00:34:27,741 --> 00:34:34,820
But if there's no tuna to take from the ocean, the enterprise and the species fails.

456
00:34:34,821 --> 00:34:39,340
So the holy grail of tuna farming becomes raising them from eggs.

457
00:34:39,341 --> 00:34:45,060
No one has perfected a method of raising bluefin that way, but progress is being made, especially

458
00:34:45,061 --> 00:34:50,380
here in South Australia.

459
00:34:50,381 --> 00:34:56,360
At Clean Seas Tuna Limited, Chairman Hagen Stair has spent several years trying to breed

460
00:34:56,361 --> 00:34:58,000
bluefin tuna in captivity.

461
00:34:58,001 --> 00:35:02,680
I heard about the propagation of fish.

462
00:35:02,681 --> 00:35:07,820
I've seen propagation of other fish when scientists were talking about it.

463
00:35:07,821 --> 00:35:13,840
And that's when we thought, we said, maybe, just maybe, by a weird chance, we can close

464
00:35:13,841 --> 00:35:18,220
the life cycle of bluefin tuna.

465
00:35:18,221 --> 00:35:24,220
Hagen has more than 20 years' experience farming bluefin in Australia and the Mediterranean.

466
00:35:24,221 --> 00:35:28,980
His desire to ensure the tuna's future is rooted in mistakes made in the past.

467
00:35:28,981 --> 00:35:30,540
We became greedy.

468
00:35:30,541 --> 00:35:35,540
We went to the Mediterranean and we were so arrogant, unfortunately.

469
00:35:35,541 --> 00:35:38,740
Everyone went tuna farming and there just isn't any tuna around.

470
00:35:38,741 --> 00:35:44,600
Now if I look five or ten years ahead, that might not be possible anymore in Australia

471
00:35:44,601 --> 00:35:47,600
either that we catch fish at sea.

472
00:35:47,601 --> 00:35:50,340
So the next thing is propagation.

473
00:35:50,341 --> 00:35:55,700
Clean Seas has already achieved success in breeding two other species of fish, kingfish

474
00:35:55,701 --> 00:35:57,700
and mulloway.

475
00:35:57,701 --> 00:36:00,120
The numbers are staggering.

476
00:36:00,121 --> 00:36:09,360
Clean Seas produces more than 4,000 tons of aquaculture bred kingfish and mulloway annually.

477
00:36:09,361 --> 00:36:12,200
Now they've turned their attention to tuna.

478
00:36:12,201 --> 00:36:20,660
In 2005, they constructed this tightly guarded hatchery in a remote Australian town at a

479
00:36:20,661 --> 00:36:24,060
cost of $18 million.

480
00:36:24,061 --> 00:36:31,100
The facility's main feature is a 790,000 gallon tank, painstakingly designed to replicate

481
00:36:31,101 --> 00:36:35,880
the optimum conditions for spawning.

482
00:36:35,881 --> 00:36:40,680
Controls are so stringent, even our cameras had to shoot at a distance.

483
00:36:40,681 --> 00:36:44,800
What we're trying to do for the tuna is, you could say we're trying to bring the ocean

484
00:36:44,801 --> 00:36:51,800
onto the land and bring the fish with it so the tuna on land, so we can produce tuna in

485
00:36:51,801 --> 00:36:54,180
an on land facility.

486
00:36:54,181 --> 00:36:59,400
Using computers, scientists can make the tank brighter or darker, approximating the movement

487
00:36:59,401 --> 00:37:02,160
of the sun.

488
00:37:02,161 --> 00:37:05,680
They can also conform the water temperature to the temperatures of the oceans where the

489
00:37:05,681 --> 00:37:07,180
tuna breed.

490
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:10,360
It's what you call manipulation of the fish.

491
00:37:10,361 --> 00:37:17,060
So you are trying to tell the fish what time of year it is and should it be spawning or

492
00:37:17,061 --> 00:37:20,940
should it be cruising or should it just be eating.

493
00:37:20,941 --> 00:37:28,740
In 2008, 16 sexually mature male and female bluefin were airlifted into the hatchery via

494
00:37:28,741 --> 00:37:29,740
helicopter.

495
00:37:29,741 --> 00:37:35,400
Four underwater cameras began capturing their every move.

496
00:37:35,401 --> 00:37:40,060
This is the laboratory for the CST breeding facility for the tuna, Southern Bluefin Tuna.

497
00:37:40,061 --> 00:37:44,480
This is where we monitor the fish, a camera system that allows us to look at the behaviour

498
00:37:44,481 --> 00:37:49,160
of the fish, how they're reacting throughout the day, after feeding, during courtship,

499
00:37:49,161 --> 00:37:53,780
as the sun comes up in the morning, as the lights go down in the afternoon for them.

500
00:37:53,781 --> 00:37:59,640
Within weeks of arrival, several pairs began exhibiting courtship behaviour.

501
00:37:59,641 --> 00:38:04,020
You can see it's quite active, the behaviour here.

502
00:38:04,021 --> 00:38:08,620
The male will align himself just behind the female's vent, and he's waiting for her to release eggs.

503
00:38:09,020 --> 00:38:15,120
So he's loaded with sperm, and as soon as any eggs are released, he's ready to release the sperm over the top of it.

504
00:38:15,520 --> 00:38:19,560
The behavior continued for months, with no tangible results.

505
00:38:20,780 --> 00:38:24,880
We'd been monitoring for the past kind of three months, looking for eggs, looking for eggs, and nothing.

506
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:26,480
Yet we had all this courtship behavior.

507
00:38:27,220 --> 00:38:28,480
Then, a breakthrough.

508
00:38:29,100 --> 00:38:32,880
You can see sperm released as a white pup in this rare Clean Seas video.

509
00:38:34,020 --> 00:38:41,060
And then sure enough we came up in the morning, had a look and there they were, we had eggs

510
00:38:41,061 --> 00:38:43,200
in the tank and it was just amazing for us.

511
00:38:43,201 --> 00:38:48,000
It was a huge accomplishment for the whole team that was involved.

512
00:38:48,001 --> 00:38:53,780
We have been able to recuperate those eggs which is also a technical feat because it's

513
00:38:53,781 --> 00:38:58,600
a big tank and we have been able to collect them, clean the eggs, incubate them and hatch

514
00:38:58,601 --> 00:39:02,640
the eggs and actually have bluefin larvae on site.

515
00:39:02,641 --> 00:39:07,200
Clean Seas managed to keep the larvae alive for five days.

516
00:39:07,201 --> 00:39:12,080
They've now joined forces with a team of scientists from Japan and are convinced they can take

517
00:39:12,081 --> 00:39:15,520
the process to the next level.

518
00:39:15,521 --> 00:39:16,520
The best is yet to come.

519
00:39:16,521 --> 00:39:18,160
We haven't even started yet.

520
00:39:18,161 --> 00:39:20,820
We will give the world some bluefin tuna.

521
00:39:20,821 --> 00:39:23,020
It's a foregone conclusion.

522
00:39:23,021 --> 00:39:29,220
If Clean Seas succeeds in raising bluefin to market size, the sky, or the sea, is the

523
00:39:29,221 --> 00:39:30,220
limit.

524
00:39:30,221 --> 00:39:33,720
not only for the bluefin, but for the other tuna species as well.

525
00:39:35,640 --> 00:39:41,540
In the meantime, experts urge consumers to take a more active role in choosing the tuna they purchase.

526
00:39:43,460 --> 00:39:48,240
Tunas are ubiquitous. They're all over the world, many, many species of tunas.

527
00:39:48,740 --> 00:39:51,220
Some, like the bluefin tuna, are in real trouble.

528
00:39:51,420 --> 00:39:54,780
But bluefin tuna is not what we eat in a tuna sandwich.

529
00:39:54,960 --> 00:39:59,640
Typical tuna sandwich are other species like skipjack and yellowfin, which are in much better shape.

530
00:40:00,220 --> 00:40:04,779
We would recommend a pole-caught or troll-caught U.S.

531
00:40:04,780 --> 00:40:08,220
West Coast or British Columbia albacore.

532
00:40:08,221 --> 00:40:13,220
Very well-managed, the catch methodology, doesn't have a lot of bycatch.

533
00:40:13,221 --> 00:40:18,220
And so if I was going to make an immediate recommendation, that's what I would do.

534
00:40:18,221 --> 00:40:22,220
People should ask their restaurateurs, they should ask their retailers,

535
00:40:22,221 --> 00:40:27,220
what kind of fish am I dealing with here? What kind of fish am I buying?

536
00:40:27,221 --> 00:40:32,220
Tuna has been part of our diet since ancient times.

537
00:40:32,221 --> 00:40:38,220
How this magnificently designed fish fares in the future is clearly in our hands.

538
00:40:38,221 --> 00:40:46,220
It's hope that through science, consumer awareness, and international cooperation,

539
00:40:46,221 --> 00:40:52,220
all tuna species will remain healthy and vibrant for millennia.

